{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1407268-what-is-the-difference-between-titans-and-primordials","id":1407268,"title":"What is the difference between Titans and Primordials?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-08-10T04:20:56-07:00","id":1407268,"title":"What is the difference between Titans and Primordials?","content":"I read the Scion corebook and I\u2019m still confused.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T06:52:43-07:00","id":1407287,"title":"","content":"So!<br /> <br /> (To start with after writing this and rereading Hero, the books actually do a really poor job explaining this as each Pantheon section seems to think a Primordial is something different. The Aesir section for instance seems to be using 1e Titanrealms weirdly. So, being confused is totally reasonable from the books.)<br /> <br /> Primordials are based on the idea in Greek texts that you have three generations of Immortals (ignoring the Nymphs for now because they're an eternal problem child and I haven't taken enough Classics classes to actually wrap my head around that), the Theoi, the Titanes, and the Protogenoi which are where the God, Titan, and Primordial division comes from. If we look at these original Greek examples we can see the distinction is purely generational, but there is the added element that the Protogenoi are 'big' concepts and the physical manifestation of this force. Eros the Elder, the Protogenoi of Attraction that is necessary for creation to exist (as without him none of the Protognoi would have had children and thus the Titans and thus the Gods), Gaia who herself is the world and her son / partner Ouranos who is the vault of heaven. These are just some of the Protogenoi (other fun figures are Tartarus, who is a person! One of the Protogenoi. Often forgotten.)<br /> <br /> With this in mind, we can see the difference between the Titans and Primordials, though ultimately these divisions are shaky at best as with all divisions in the game as Scion's chasis is an imperfect model of the world it represents. You can't make an entire game system for each individual Pantheon, so you make due with one that more or less works for everyone with minimal jank. The main differences we see here are that Primordials are the following: Oldest Things in Creation, Manifestations of Specific Concepts, Overwhelmingly Powerful, Supremely Withdrawn. (Comparatively, Titans are: Sometimes Antagonistic to a) the Pantheon b) Humanity c) Both, Imprisoned, Embodiments of Specific Concepts, Powerful, Semi-Distant) though no figure needs to hit all of these 'beats' to 'qualify'.<br /> <br /> The other factor that we have seen is all Overgods that have appeared in Scion are Primordials (except for Re but I believe that is an error on the part of Scion's internal logic due to the general issues that the Netjer chapter presents).<br /> <br /> In general, Primordials are these distant, fundamental beings that are the first entities in reality and are potentially more powerful than the most powerful God in their Pantheon, up to the extent that they may be an Overgod, a God of Gods. They are incredibly distant most of the time however (though exceptions exist) and we tend to see their influence through other figures rather than personal intervention.<br /> <br /> So! Some examples.<br /> - Gaia is a great example of a Primordial who is a raw physical manifestation of a force. She is the world, she is one of (if not the, depending on the tale) oldest beings in reality for her Pantheon,<br /> - Ymir is another similar example, except he is also dead. One of the oldest beings in reality, he was butchered to create the nine worlds from his corpse by Odin and his brothers.<br /> - NA\u00f1k\u00ee\u2019lsLas is a different example of a Primordial, the Haida Storyteller. A being who lives beneath the ocean and tells the story of reality as it unfolds. His words are reality, everything that is, is unfolding as he says it. He created the world by telling the story of the creation of the world. He is <i>supremely</i> powerful, distant, withdrawn, and fundamental for the world to 'work'.<br /> - Re is a good example as well. He is the source of other Gods, who are manifested out of him. Re existing causes the world to exist (and when he blinks reality will end), and like the light from the sun, various Netjer exist as an emanation of his self.<br /> - Nyame from the Abosom (the Pantheon of the Ashanti people, the Pantheon Anansi is from) is the only true God in his Cosmology, with all the beings we would identify as 'Gods' as being lesser Supernatural Beings who recieve power directly from him, and he can cut them off at a whim if it is being used in a way he disapproves of. He is All Powerful, an incredibly nice guy, but very withdrawn from the world after an unfortunate incident where he kept being hit in the head when he was trying to take a direct hand helping humanity.<br /> <br /> The problem children, as always, are the T\u00faatha D\u00e9 Danann. If you want a really long explanation of this the expansion in my signature has a chapter on this IIRC. But, more or less, the T\u00faatha D\u00e9 Primordials, as accounted in the book, are Danu and Fintan mac Brocha (called the Salmon of Wisdom there somewhat erroneously, it appears the author mixed up some things) who are not All Powerful, are not manifestations of anything, or any other Primordial qualifier. They are just the oldest beings left around still and are part of the ancestors of the various tribal groups that make up Ireland's mythological tale of invasions. So, the T\u00faatha D\u00e9 seem to, once again, be off doing their own thing.<br /> <br /> I hope that helps!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T06:57:51-07:00","id":1407288,"title":"","content":"The lines between Titans and Primordials (and the gods) is meant to be pretty fuzzy.<br /> <br /> In a more mechanical sense, Primordials are gods, but they're tied literally to grand concepts that makes it so they don't interact with The World like other gods do, and thus generally do not have Scions. Titans are not-gods but something related to them, that embody Purviews, and since they are tied more metaphorically to their natures they can more easily influence the World.<br /> <br /> A Primordial of the Earth can only exist in The World as the literal planet... which means they can't really do much besides sit there and have stuff happen on them. They can only manifest and distinct beings in The Overworld. A Titan of the Earth is the Earth Purview, allowing them to be the Earth itself, as well as 'smaller' things that allow more interaction with what's going on arouned them.<br /> <br /> In a more in-universe political sense, the Primordials either sided with the gods in the Titanomachy, or stayed out of it, and the Titans are the ones that opposed the gods.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T07:06:28-07:00","id":1407289,"title":"","content":"Thank you both \ud83d\ude0a. That really helps.<br /> <br /> I think Re definitely sounds like a Primordial.<br /> <br /> They\u2019re doing a whole book on the Titanomachy, right?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T08:17:43-07:00","id":1407298,"title":"","content":"I'd add that while Titans are much more defined by their antagonistic relationship to the Pantheons, Primordials can also on occasion be antagonistic. Gaia herself is the best example of that. But as Heavy Arms points out, her very nature as the earth seems to keep her from engaging in a battle with the Gods herself - she always acts through intermediaries, Typhoneus, the Gigantes, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10624-sacerdos","name":"Sacerdos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10624_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"231"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T08:42:59-07:00","id":1407304,"title":"","content":"I will say that, while in theory the books say that Primordials can't manifest directly, they use several example that actually just live in The World as normal people. For instance, the Salmon of Knowledge / Fintan mac Brocha is just a dude who hangs around and watches stuff and gets in arguments with birds.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T09:00:56-07:00","id":1407307,"title":"","content":"My impressions on this are:<br /> Gods have worshipers, what make their myths the center of the entire mythology. Some gods have less direct worshipping, but they are treated as \u201cpart of the team\u201d by the other gods.<br /> Titans have little to no worshipers, and the ones that worship them usually don\u2019t do it by respect, but by fear or simple desire of power.<br /> Primordials don\u2019t have worshipers at all, because they don\u2019t interact or don\u2019t care much about people or because they are too linked to one aspect of the universe to do anything else.<br /> <br /> its a bit simplistic, but it is general as I see.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/en/product/481584/house-rules-bundle-bundle\" target=\"_blank\">House Rules - The Bundle</a> - House Rules for Everyone<br /> <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/en/product/481581/powered-inspiration-bundle\" target=\"_blank\">Powered Inspired</a> - Powered Characters using Inspiration and Gifts<br /> <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/413362/Fists-and-Tomes-BUNDLE\" target=\"_blank\">Fists and Tomes</a> - Inspired Martial Arts and Mysticism for Talents<br /> <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/en/product/492147/supertech\" target=\"_blank\">Supertech</a> - Super Science in the hands of Talents","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24985-mateus-luz","name":"Mateus Luz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24985_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2950"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T10:05:56-07:00","id":1407319,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1407268-what-is-the-difference-between-titans-and-primordials?p=1407289#post1407289\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> They\u2019re doing a whole book on the Titanomachy, right?</div> </div> </div> </div>I believe the intention to do so has been announced. But it could be a while, and as always, things may chance due to circumstances.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T11:45:41-07:00","id":1407342,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1407268-what-is-the-difference-between-titans-and-primordials?p=1407319#post1407319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I believe the intention to do so has been announced. But it could be a while, and as always, things may chance due to circumstances.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's currently in layout, so as these things go it's not that far off. Give it a few months.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T13:38:52-07:00","id":1407361,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mateus Luz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1407268-what-is-the-difference-between-titans-and-primordials?p=1407307#post1407307\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My impressions on this are:<br /> Gods have worshipers, what make their myths the center of the entire mythology. Some gods have less direct worshipping, but they are treated as \u201cpart of the team\u201d by the other gods.<br /> Titans have little to no worshipers, and the ones that worship them usually don\u2019t do it by respect, but by fear or simple desire of power.<br /> Primordials don\u2019t have worshipers at all, because they don\u2019t interact or don\u2019t care much about people or because they are too linked to one aspect of the universe to do anything else.<br /> <br /> its a bit simplistic, but it is general as I see.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think that really pans out, considering that I know there's at least one Asura I know of who has a huge following and would very much like him to replace Indra early, and by most accounts he's running his own kingdom and doing a pretty good job.<br /> <br /> Honestly, I view the divisions as mostly political and there's no ironclad definition since it may vary between Pantheons. My general guidelines are:<br /> <br /> Gods - Gods<br /> Titans - Nominal Enemies of the Gods. Division may be innate nature or political.<br /> Primordials - Big. Probably very distant. Often Old and occasionally the Previous Generation, sometimes the ones who went &quot;There's a lot of Nothing here&quot; and then there's the World.<br /> <br /> Are there exceptions? Heck yeah. Turns out most religions throughout history didn't communicate and agree upon a unified cosmology, so I'd take it case by case. Which you kind of have to do with a lot of Pantheons anyway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":2142,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","user":"Kyman201","date":". 08-10-2020, 05:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T14:29:00-07:00","id":1407374,"title":"","content":"True. Every pantheon is different.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T14:47:01-07:00","id":1407381,"title":"","content":"A thing to keep in mind is that the God-Titan-Primordial as used in the books are very much game terms.<br /> <br /> And while the Theoi would use those terms, the Aesir wont call Surtr a Titan. Its a Jotun from Muspelheim.<br /> <br /> When in-universe try as much as possible to use the in-pantheon term for whichever entity is involved.<br /> As the Storyguide you can use the God-Titan-Primordial as dhortcuts to explain the nature of the entity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Completed campaign: Scion 2nd Edition. Les L\u00e9gendes Currently playing: Being a dad for a 3 year old daughter and a 2 years old son and now a beautiful new baby.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11312-maitrecorbo","name":"Maitrecorbo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11312_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"719"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T15:01:36-07:00","id":1407385,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maitrecorbo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1407268-what-is-the-difference-between-titans-and-primordials?p=1407381#post1407381\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A thing to keep in mind is that the God-Titan-Primordial as used in the books are very much game terms.<br /> <br /> And while the Theoi would use those terms, the Aesir wont call Surtr a Titan. Its a Jotun from Muspelheim.<br /> <br /> When in-universe try as much as possible to use the in-pantheon term for whichever entity is involved.<br /> As the Storyguide you can use the God-Titan-Primordial as dhortcuts to explain the nature of the entity.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you. I forgot about that. That makes a lot of sense.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-11T02:30:13-07:00","id":1407470,"title":"","content":"Generation, isn\u2019t it?<br /> <br /> In the Classic Greek myth, for example, Primordials (note: Prime means \u2018first\u2019) are the first generation, followed by their children, the Titans, followed by the Titans\u2019 offspring, the Olympians. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2517-trippy","name":"Trippy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2517_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1018"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-11T11:14:38-07:00","id":1407556,"title":"","content":"I mean that tracks if you're only looking at the Theoi, but the generational thing doesn't always apply for other Pantheons. See my snark about how humanity didn't agree on a unified cosmology.<br /> <br /> Thus my comment of how I look for aspects like &quot;Big. Often distant. Sometimes are the ones who created everything&quot;<br /> <br /> Unless they're the Tuatha where it seems like the book is going by the progenitor of the People are the Primordials, to say nothing of how Brahma is written as a Primordial in the Deva section (and IMO if Brahma's a primordial, then Shiva and Vishnu should be as well)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-11T12:19:00-07:00","id":1407567,"title":"","content":"Well, the issue is one of definition I guess, but the terms \u2018Primordial\u2019 and \u2018Titan\u2019 come from the Greek myths. I am not yet an expert on Scion as a game, but I do note that not all Pantheons actually have Titans - only some do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2517-trippy","name":"Trippy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2517_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1018"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-11T12:37:23-07:00","id":1407569,"title":"","content":"Yeah, someone else in the thread noted that the individual Pantheons probably don't in-universe call their enemies Titans. The Teotl and the Deva, despite being very dedicated to fighting their enemies, probably don't call the Asura or the Tzitzimimeh 'Titans' in-universe, since they already have names.<br /> <br /> Sort of like how technically there's a difference between what the kami would call a god (which is a multitude of beings) and what Scion calls God-tier Gods (and thus viable Scion patrons).<br /> <br /> So yeah, my notes on the Titan vs Primordial divide is &quot;It's not clear cut at all, try to take guesses based on the individual feel and role in the cosmology, sometimes they won't work and there'll be exceptions, I recommend just rolling with this and embracing the madness&quot;<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-11T12:40:29-07:00","id":1407570,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyman201</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1407268-what-is-the-difference-between-titans-and-primordials?p=1407556#post1407556\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean that tracks if you're only looking at the Theoi, but the generational thing doesn't always apply for other Pantheons. See my snark about how humanity didn't agree on a unified cosmology.<br /> <br /> Thus my comment of how I look for aspects like &amp;quot;Big. Often distant. Sometimes are the ones who created everything&amp;quot;<br /> <br /> Unless they're the Tuatha where it seems like the book is going by the progenitor of the People are the Primordials, to say nothing of how Brahma is written as a Primordial in the Deva section (and IMO if Brahma's a primordial, then Shiva and Vishnu should be as well)</div> </div> </div> </div>Good point about Vishnu and Shiva.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-11T12:45:39-07:00","id":1407571,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1407268-what-is-the-difference-between-titans-and-primordials?p=1407570#post1407570\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Good point about Vishnu and Shiva.</div> </div> </div> </div>Now note that being Primordials won't stop Brahma, Vishnu, and Shiva from having Scions and being Patrons. As Watcher noted, the 'Don't often manifest in the World' is more what you call a guideline that isn't always followed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-11T13:32:43-07:00","id":1407577,"title":"","content":"Okay. Thanks for clarifying. That makes sense.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-11T18:17:11-07:00","id":1407630,"title":"","content":"Yeah I notice here and there some people go &quot;But you can't be a Scion of a Primordial&quot; to which my answer is &quot;Why not?&quot; and noting that Kichi Manitou is a Primordial (and then adding in how Ra, Shiva, and Vishnu probably SHOULD be) and statted up in the Manitou section as a viable Patron, and like literally no part of the book says that you can't be a Scion of a Primordial.<br /> <br /> They may be RARE but heck, that's ripe for story opportunity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-11T18:31:38-07:00","id":1407636,"title":"","content":"I still think Re is or should be a Primordial. He is the creator of the Netjer pantheon (and according to them the World).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-12T02:01:21-07:00","id":1407670,"title":"","content":"This is all interesting food for thought...<br /> <br /> Might we say a better criterion for what separates a Titan-class divinity from a Primordial-class divinity is their origin? A Primordial springs from nothing/chaos whereas a Titan is born of (insert personified thing or things here)? In this case Re would be a Titan if you consider Nuen, Heh, Kekui, and Qerh to be Primordials, otherwise you can add him as a Primordial if you discount the Ogdoad.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/765-darksol-aeternium","name":"Darksol-aeternium","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar765_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"392"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-12T05:14:25-07:00","id":1407685,"title":"","content":"Hmm. That\u2019s interesting. I didn\u2019t think about the Ogdoad.<br /> <br /> I think a lot of times Titans are the children of Primordials and gods are the children of Titans, but I don\u2019t think that\u2019s universally true.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-12T08:41:25-07:00","id":1407703,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1407268-what-is-the-difference-between-titans-and-primordials?p=1407685#post1407685\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm. That\u2019s interesting. I didn\u2019t think about the Ogdoad.<br /> <br /> I think a lot of times Titans are the children of Primordials and gods are the children of Titans, but I don\u2019t think that\u2019s universally true.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think that's very true at all outside of the Greek gods.<br /> <br /> The Deva aren't the children of the Asura, the Yazata aren't children of Angra Mainyu, the Tuatha aren't related to the Fomorians.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-12T09:09:38-07:00","id":1407706,"title":"","content":"Thanks. Idk that. So the generation thing is primarily a Greek thing?<br /> <br /> What about the Netjer? Aren\u2019t they all descended from Re?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-12T13:39:15-07:00","id":1407766,"title":"","content":"I'm not as well-read with Egyptian myth, alas. But like, something to keep in mind is that trying to find a more rigid pattern defining Gods, Titans, and Primordials is gonna break down between Pantheons, because their structures don't always mesh.<br /> <br /> That's why my advice is viewing God/Titan/Primordial divides as being very much seen internally within Pantheons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-12T17:35:26-07:00","id":1407821,"title":"","content":"I\u2019m not an expert at Scion, but based on what I know I totally agree with your analysis.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-12T21:29:21-07:00","id":1407862,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1407268-what-is-the-difference-between-titans-and-primordials?p=1407706#post1407706\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks. Idk that. So the generation thing is primarily a Greek thing?<br /> <br /> What about the Netjer? Aren\u2019t they all descended from Re?</div> </div> </div> </div>The terms \u2018Primordials\u2019, \u2018Titans\u2019 and the Gods (\u201cOlympians\u201d) is <i>exclusively</i> a Greek thing. However, Scion has adopted these terms for a broader use in relation to all Pantheons. As we can see, however, they don\u2019t exactly fit because not every Pantheon has myths based on a succession of godly generations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2517-trippy","name":"Trippy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2517_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1018"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-13T05:00:14-07:00","id":1407896,"title":"","content":"Got it. Thanks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-14T15:26:47-07:00","id":1408257,"title":"","content":"God/Titan relationships are probably the most varied thing between pantheons honestly. Except for a few exceptions (looking at you Tuatha...) most Primordials can be pretty easy to define. They are all &quot;Big&quot;. If a Deity is in some way seen as being older, primal, all-encompassing, distant, or the like, it's a fair bet that they may be a primordial rather than a God.<br /> <br /> Titans are generally much more contextual to their pantheons though. (and sometimes can comprise multiple groups within a given pantheon). Sometimes they're an older generation that the current Gods are descended from. This is most notable for the Theoi and thus it's easy to treat it as a default, but you also see it with the Manitou, wherein Ae-pungishimook, the West Wind, is considered the father of Winonah's children, who comprise most of the pantheon. Notably however both these pantheons typically attribute some sort of sin of the previous generation that justifies why they have taken prominence instead. (With the Theoi it's that there's a cyclical clash every generation. Oranos is castrated by Kronus who is in turn chopped apart by Zeus, who now stands vigilant for the son fated to overthrow him. Meanwhile Ae-pungishimook is often attributed as conceiving Winonah's children by rape, which leaves them largely estranged from their Titanic father.)<br /> <br /> Other times Titans serve more as an antagonistic counterpart to the Gods, more akin to a rival family than a previous generation. The Jotun of Norse Myth, the Fomoire of Irish Legend most easily fit this archetype, Asura sometimes can be considered to fall into this category as well. Notably, the conflicts between Gods and Titans of this type aren't always drawn on grand ideological lines, while the titans of this variety may often have more malevolent characteristics that may lead them to conflict with the Gods, just as often the conflict is more political. Because of this it's not uncommon for Gods to mingle more freely with Titans of this variety, often intermarrying with them or the line blurring sufficiently that a figure that is cosmologically a Titan can effectively be considered a God in mechanical terms (Loki and Skadi are both technically Jotun for instance, despite being largely tolerated by the Aesir, and some of the Asura are considered virtuous enough that one is actually set to step in as the new Indra once he gets replaced according to the grand plan of the Trimurti)<br /> <br /> This brings us to the fundamental threat. Some titans are defined by the fact that they present some sort of concrete threat to the Gods and/or creation itself. They may or may not be depicted as matching the Gods in power, but they are always considered to be dangerous enough on a level that demands attention. This usually means that they are not treated with kid gloves like other titans may sometimes be, though it's not impossible for them to still have a relationship with Gods on occasion. The Daeva and Angra Mainyu are perhaps the most obvious example of this. While the Yazata believe that they will eventually triumph over them, the two groups still believe they are locked in a conflict between Good and Evil. There are also the Tzitzimime, the Star Demons that are held back from devouring all life on Earth by the sacrifices made to the Teotl. Notably sometimes some of the Asura and Jotun can drift into these roles. Loki and his children Fenris Wolf and Jormungand are fated to contribute to Ragnarok and the death of the Gods along with Surtr of the Fire Jotun, while often many of the Asura are considered to transgress against the ways of virtue to an extent that they are considered more fundamental threat then rival clan. Fundamental threats also often include more bestial threats that are barely considered free-thinking entities but are still dangerous enough that they fall into this category, often dangerous primarily because they cannot be effectively negotiated with. Apep is the prime example, a snake formed from Ra's Umbilical cord that pursues him each night attempting to devour him and thus end all life. The Netjer have a good track record thus far in dealing with him, but it's an ongoing struggle. Niddhogg, gnawing at the roots of the World tree and contributing to Ragnarok, Tlaltecuhtli the Hungry Earth of Aztec myth, and possibly Typhon, Gaia's Draconic vengence against the Theoi, also fall into that category (though Typhon is usually at least somewhat sapient.)<br /> <br /> And then there are Monsters. These are entities that, despite often being dangerous, usually aren't powerful enough to be quite so fundamental a threat as those above. Because their threat isn't fundamental, the Gods can often negotiate with them without so much being at stake, and thus they sometimes act on tolerable terms like a rival clan. The Demons that the Shen and Kami deal with typically fall into this category, along with the Rakshasa of Hinduism. On rare occasions one of these beings can become powerful enough that they might encroach onto the level of one of these other categories, but it's still usually not considered such a pressing threat in their cosmology. Sun Wukong is perhaps the closest to make the leap to another category in Shen mythology, as prior to being a God, he was a powerful Demon Monkey that nearly upended the whole Celestial order of heaven largely out of boredom. However even he was eventually put in his place (with the help of the Buddha and a Journey to the West). This category is also the closest to the Orisha get to having Titans. Notably the Orisha don't have figures that get anywhere near the category of a God that aren't benevolent servents of Olodumare, their Overgod figure. However the Orisha do believe that humans can become witches that are responsible for malevolent activities, but even then such beings are not truly threats to the Orisha themselves, only threats for their worshippers. Most pantheons also have all manner of monsterous creatures abounding, but most are threats primarily to Scions, not to Gods.<br /> <br /> <br /> Well this post got long, but I hope that it was helpful all the same.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24666-ravian","name":"Ravian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"46"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-14T17:02:30-07:00","id":1408284,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ravian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1407268-what-is-the-difference-between-titans-and-primordials?p=1408257#post1408257\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">God/Titan relationships are probably the most varied thing between pantheons honestly. Except for a few exceptions (looking at you Tuatha...) most Primordials can be pretty easy to define. They are all &amp;quot;Big&amp;quot;. If a Deity is in some way seen as being older, primal, all-encompassing, distant, or the like, it's a fair bet that they may be a primordial rather than a God.<br /> <br /> Titans are generally much more contextual to their pantheons though. (and sometimes can comprise multiple groups within a given pantheon). Sometimes they're an older generation that the current Gods are descended from. This is most notable for the Theoi and thus it's easy to treat it as a default, but you also see it with the Manitou, wherein Ae-pungishimook, the West Wind, is considered the father of Winonah's children, who comprise most of the pantheon. Notably however both these pantheons typically attribute some sort of sin of the previous generation that justifies why they have taken prominence instead. (With the Theoi it's that there's a cyclical clash every generation. Oranos is castrated by Kronus who is in turn chopped apart by Zeus, who now stands vigilant for the son fated to overthrow him. Meanwhile Ae-pungishimook is often attributed as conceiving Winonah's children by rape, which leaves them largely estranged from their Titanic father.)<br /> <br /> Other times Titans serve more as an antagonistic counterpart to the Gods, more akin to a rival family than a previous generation. The Jotun of Norse Myth, the Fomoire of Irish Legend most easily fit this archetype, Asura sometimes can be considered to fall into this category as well. Notably, the conflicts between Gods and Titans of this type aren't always drawn on grand ideological lines, while the titans of this variety may often have more malevolent characteristics that may lead them to conflict with the Gods, just as often the conflict is more political. Because of this it's not uncommon for Gods to mingle more freely with Titans of this variety, often intermarrying with them or the line blurring sufficiently that a figure that is cosmologically a Titan can effectively be considered a God in mechanical terms (Loki and Skadi are both technically Jotun for instance, despite being largely tolerated by the Aesir, and some of the Asura are considered virtuous enough that one is actually set to step in as the new Indra once he gets replaced according to the grand plan of the Trimurti)<br /> <br /> This brings us to the fundamental threat. Some titans are defined by the fact that they present some sort of concrete threat to the Gods and/or creation itself. They may or may not be depicted as matching the Gods in power, but they are always considered to be dangerous enough on a level that demands attention. This usually means that they are not treated with kid gloves like other titans may sometimes be, though it's not impossible for them to still have a relationship with Gods on occasion. The Daeva and Angra Mainyu are perhaps the most obvious example of this. While the Yazata believe that they will eventually triumph over them, the two groups still believe they are locked in a conflict between Good and Evil. There are also the Tzitzimime, the Star Demons that are held back from devouring all life on Earth by the sacrifices made to the Teotl. Notably sometimes some of the Asura and Jotun can drift into these roles. Loki and his children Fenris Wolf and Jormungand are fated to contribute to Ragnarok and the death of the Gods along with Surtr of the Fire Jotun, while often many of the Asura are considered to transgress against the ways of virtue to an extent that they are considered more fundamental threat then rival clan. Fundamental threats also often include more bestial threats that are barely considered free-thinking entities but are still dangerous enough that they fall into this category, often dangerous primarily because they cannot be effectively negotiated with. Apep is the prime example, a snake formed from Ra's Umbilical cord that pursues him each night attempting to devour him and thus end all life. The Netjer have a good track record thus far in dealing with him, but it's an ongoing struggle. Niddhogg, gnawing at the roots of the World tree and contributing to Ragnarok, Tlaltecuhtli the Hungry Earth of Aztec myth, and possibly Typhon, Gaia's Draconic vengence against the Theoi, also fall into that category (though Typhon is usually at least somewhat sapient.)<br /> <br /> And then there are Monsters. These are entities that, despite often being dangerous, usually aren't powerful enough to be quite so fundamental a threat as those above. Because their threat isn't fundamental, the Gods can often negotiate with them without so much being at stake, and thus they sometimes act on tolerable terms like a rival clan. The Demons that the Shen and Kami deal with typically fall into this category, along with the Rakshasa of Hinduism. On rare occasions one of these beings can become powerful enough that they might encroach onto the level of one of these other categories, but it's still usually not considered such a pressing threat in their cosmology. Sun Wukong is perhaps the closest to make the leap to another category in Shen mythology, as prior to being a God, he was a powerful Demon Monkey that nearly upended the whole Celestial order of heaven largely out of boredom. However even he was eventually put in his place (with the help of the Buddha and a Journey to the West). This category is also the closest to the Orisha get to having Titans. Notably the Orisha don't have figures that get anywhere near the category of a God that aren't benevolent servents of Olodumare, their Overgod figure. However the Orisha do believe that humans can become witches that are responsible for malevolent activities, but even then such beings are not truly threats to the Orisha themselves, only threats for their worshippers. Most pantheons also have all manner of monsterous creatures abounding, but most are threats primarily to Scions, not to Gods.<br /> <br /> <br /> Well this post got long, but I hope that it was helpful all the same.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you!!!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]}]}